#---
# purpose: "Enumerable.reduce halts on :halt and runs the reducer it was handed, whatever order the dispatch arms arrived in"
#---
#
# ## What this fixture pins
#
# Two `Enumerable.reduce/3` calls over one range, with two different reducer
# lambdas. The compiler shares one `Range.reduce_cont/6` body between them, and
# its step callsite settles two targets of `Range.reduce_step/6`:
#
#     arm0  (..., {:cont, int} | {:halt, int}, #66closure[])
#     arm1  (..., {:cont, int},                #68closure[])
#
# The state column used to be ONE question to the runtime: `RuntimeTypePredicate`
# recorded a tuple's arity and nothing else, so `{:cont, int}` and
# `{:cont, int} | {:halt, int}` both projected to "a 2-tuple" and `{:halt, 3}`
# passed arm1's test as readily as arm0's. The reducer column was what really
# separated the two arms, and `runtime_type_test_envelope` used to erase both
# literals to `fun_top` -- so no test the plan could emit told them apart, and
# nothing but arm order decided which body ran. Arm order is the scheduler's.
#
# BOTH columns are real questions now. fz-kdt.125 gave the predicate a callable
# axis, and fz-kdt.119 gave the tuple axis a sub-predicate per position, so the
# `:cont`/`:halt` tag separates the states as well. This fixture keeps its
# value as the reducer-column pin -- line 2 is what fails if the callable
# position stops being asked -- and it now also holds the tag column.
#
# Line 1 is what that costs when the order goes the other way: with the narrow
# arm first, `{:halt, 3}` lands in the body specialized on `{:cont, int}`, the
# halt is read as a continue, and the reduce reports `{:done, 3}` instead of
# `{:halted, 3}`. fz-kdt.125 made the reducer column a real question -- a
# closure value's heap word names the lambda it was minted from -- so both arms
# stay and each is reached only by the values its own reducer travelled with.
# No order can swallow the halt, because no order decides anything here.
#
# Line 2 is the other half. `1500` is `100 + 200 + 300 + 400 + 500`: the
# reducer the value carried is the one that ran. Were the callable position
# lowered as a grounded direct call to some other body's closure literal, this
# line would read `{:done, 15}`.
#
fn main() do
  dbg(Enumerable.reduce(1..5, {:cont, 0}, fn (x, acc) ->
    if x > 2 do
      {:halt, acc}
    else
      {:cont, acc + x}
    end
  end))
  dbg(Enumerable.reduce(1..5, {:cont, 0}, fn (x, acc) -> {:cont, acc + x * 100} end))
end
